        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "example_question",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Simplify the expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Simplify the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
            "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>First, let&#x27;s recall the secant-tangent identity:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 1+\\tan^2 \\theta = \\sec^2{\\theta}]</span></p><p>We rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta}]</span></p><p>We now cancel the common factor of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\sec^2 \\theta]</span> in the numerator and denominator and simplify the fraction:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (2\\sec^{2} \u03b8)/(\\sec^{2} \u03b8)|= (2\\sec^{2} \u03b8)/(\\sec^{2} \u03b8); = (2 \u22c5 1)/(1); = 2]</span></p><p>Therefore, we conclude that</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = 2.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n[MATH: 1+\\tan^2 \\theta = \\sec^2{\\theta}]\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta}]\n\nWe now cancel the common factor of [MATH: \\sec^2 \\theta] in the numerator and denominator and simplify the fraction:\n\n[MATH: (2\\sec^{2} \u03b8)/(\\sec^{2} \u03b8)|= (2\\sec^{2} \u03b8)/(\\sec^{2} \u03b8); = (2 \u22c5 1)/(1); = 2]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = 2.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Example Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n[MATH: 1+\\tan^2 \\theta = \\sec^2{\\theta}]\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta}]\n\nWe now cancel the common factor of [MATH: \\sec^2 \\theta] in the numerator and denominator and simplify the fraction:\n\n[MATH: (2\\sec^{2} \u03b8)/(\\sec^{2} \u03b8)|= (2\\sec^{2} \u03b8)/(\\sec^{2} \u03b8); = (2 \u22c5 1)/(1); = 2]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = 2.]"
      },
