          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>First, let&#x27;s recall the secant-tangent identity:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}]</span></p><p>Also, recall the following identity:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tan x\\cdot \\cot x = 1]</span></p><p>Using the above identities, we can simplify our expression as follows:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2\\tan x \u22c5 (\\cot x + \\tan x)|= 2\\tan x \u22c5 \\cot x + 2\\tan^{2} x; = 2 \u22c5 1 + 2\\tan^{2} x; = 2(1 + \\tan^{2} x); = 2\\sec^{2} x]</span></p><p>Therefore, we conclude that</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2\\tan x\\cdot(\\cot x+\\tan{x})= 2\\sec^2x.]</span> </p>",
            "readable_text": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n[MATH: 1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}]\n\nAlso, recall the following identity:\n\n[MATH: \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tan x\\cdot \\cot x = 1]\n\nUsing the above identities, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n[MATH: 2\\tan x \u22c5 (\\cot x + \\tan x)|= 2\\tan x \u22c5 \\cot x + 2\\tan^{2} x; = 2 \u22c5 1 + 2\\tan^{2} x; = 2(1 + \\tan^{2} x); = 2\\sec^{2} x]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: 2\\tan x\\cdot(\\cot x+\\tan{x})= 2\\sec^2x.]",
