    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nLet's recall the Pythagorean identity:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\cos^{2} x + \\sin^{2} x = 1]\n\nContent:\nIf we divide both sides of this identity by [MATH: {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x},] we get the equation\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac {\\cos^2 x} {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} + \\dfrac {\\sin^2 x}{\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} = \\dfrac 1 {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}]\n\nContent:\nwhich can be written as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 1+ \\left(\\dfrac {\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac 1 {\\cos x}\\right)^2 \\qquad (\\ast)]\n\nContent:\nNow, recall the following identities:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}, \\qquad \\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}]\n\nContent:\nSubstituting the above identities into [MATH: (\\ast)], we get\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 1 + ((\\sin x)/(\\cos x))^{2}|= ((1)/(\\cos x))^{2}; 1 + (\\tan x)^{2}|= (\\sec x)^{2}]\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 1 + \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x.]\n\nContent:\nThe final identity is called the secant-tangent identity. We often use it to simplify trigonometric expressions containing both tangent and secant functions.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent Identity: Replacing a Squared Tangent\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n[MATH: 1+\\tan^2 \\theta = \\sec^2{\\theta}]\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta}]\n\nWe now cancel the common factor of [MATH: \\sec^2 \\theta] in the numerator and denominator and simplify the fraction:\n\n[MATH: (2\\sec^{2} \u03b8)/(\\sec^{2} \u03b8)|= (2\\sec^{2} \u03b8)/(\\sec^{2} \u03b8); = (2 \u22c5 1)/(1); = 2]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{2\\sec^2 \\theta}{1+\\tan^2 \\theta} = 2.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\\tan \u03b8 + 1)^{2} \u2212 2\\tan \u03b8 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \\sec \u03b8]\nb. [MATH: \\sec \u03b8 \u2212 2]\nc. [MATH: 2\\sec \u03b8]\nd. [MATH: \\sec^{2} \u03b8]\ne. [MATH: 2\\sec^{2} \u03b8]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (1 + \\tan^{2} \u03b8)/(\\sec^{2} \u03b8) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2\\sec^{2} \u03b8]\nb. [MATH: 2\\sec \u03b8]\nc. [MATH: 1]\nd. [MATH: 0]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent Identity: Replacing a Squared Secant\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: (1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x )] in terms of [MATH: \\tan x] only.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n[MATH: 1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}]\n\nFirst, we expand the parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n[MATH: (1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x ) = 1 - \\sec^2x]\n\nThen, we rewrite the expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n[MATH: 1 \u2212 \\sec^{2} x|= 1 \u2212 (1 + \\tan^{2} x); = 1 \u2212 1 \u2212 \\tan^{2} x; = \u2212 \\tan^{2} x]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: (1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x )= -\\tan^2 x.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \\sec^{2} x \u2212 2\\tan^{2} x =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 1 + \\tan^{2} x]\nb. [MATH: 1 \u2212 \\tan^{2} x]\nc. [MATH: \\tan^{2} x \u2212 1]\nd. [MATH: 1 + \\tan x]\ne. [MATH: 1 \u2212 \\tan x]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\\sec^{2} x \u2212 1)/(2\\tan x) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2\\tan x]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 2\\tan x]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 \\tan x]\nd. [MATH: \\tan x]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(2)\\tan x]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent and Other Identities\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: 2\\tan x\\cdot(\\cot x+\\tan{x})] in terms of [MATH: \\sec x] only.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n[MATH: 1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}]\n\nAlso, recall the following identity:\n\n[MATH: \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tan x\\cdot \\cot x = 1]\n\nUsing the above identities, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n[MATH: 2\\tan x \u22c5 (\\cot x + \\tan x)|= 2\\tan x \u22c5 \\cot x + 2\\tan^{2} x; = 2 \u22c5 1 + 2\\tan^{2} x; = 2(1 + \\tan^{2} x); = 2\\sec^{2} x]\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n[MATH: 2\\tan x\\cdot(\\cot x+\\tan{x})= 2\\sec^2x.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (1 \u2212 \\sin^{2} x) \u22c5 (1 + \\tan^{2} x) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \\cos x]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 1]\nc. [MATH: 1]\nd. [MATH: \\cos^{2} x]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(\\cos x)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\\tan \u03b8 \u22c5 \\sec \u03b8)/(1 + \\tan^{2} \u03b8) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \\sin \u03b8]\nb. [MATH: 1 \u2212 \\sin \u03b8]\nc. [MATH: \\sin^{2} \u03b8]\nd. [MATH: \\sin^{2} \u03b8 + 1]\ne. [MATH: 1 + \\sin \u03b8]"
